A launched session also cannot launch another, so `run`, `draft --runner` and
`review --runner` refuse there too: report what you found and let the operator
start the next one. Everything not listed here is safe from a session,
`audit` included — a review session is legitimately independent of the session
that implemented the work.

### What a supervising session may do

`drover supervise` launches one session with `DROVER_ACTOR=supervisor` to watch
drives and handle what comes up. It answers to the table above, except that it
may run `drive` while `enqueue` is `supervisor` or `auto`; the sessions that
drive launches are agents. It also answers to this project's `[supervise]`
policy: a command that is one of these actions runs only at `supervisor` or
`auto`, and refuses with exit 3 otherwise. Here a supervisor may not run
{{ range $i, $c := .SupervisorRefused }}{{ if $i }}, {{ end }}{{ $c }}{{ end }}.

| Action | Policy | Ceiling | What it is |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
{{- range .Policies }}
| `{{ .Key }}` | {{ .Policy }}{{ if not .Declared }} (default){{ end }} | {{ .Ceiling }} | {{ .Description }} |
{{- end }}

`auto`: drive does it. `supervisor`: a supervising session may do it, and
reports it. `operator`: drover stops, notifies, and waits for a person. `off`:
nothing happens and the park stands. An agent session never acts on this table;
it is here so you know whom a park belongs to.

The check reads an environment variable drover sets when it launches you, or,
where that is unset, the `.drover/session` marker drover leaves in the tree a
session works in. It is a guardrail against running a command you read in a code
block, not a boundary: if you find yourself wanting to strip it, that is the
signal to stop and report instead. `DROVER_ACTOR=operator` is the opt-out a
person exports to steer from inside a sealed tree, never one an agent takes.

Exit codes: `0` clean, `1` drover could not answer, `2` findings against the
project (`check`), `3` a rule drover enforces refused the operation.

`--json` is on `status`, `show`, `unaudited`, `drive`, `drives`, `attach`, `check`, `follow-ups`,
`intake --list`, `projects`, `skills`, `t3 discover` and `report`. Parse that, never the table.

