## Work-item completion review, triggered by "<id> finished" (or similar)

When the operator says an item finished, run this loop for that id without
waiting for further instruction.

**Start with `drover review <id>`.** It assembles the prompt: the numbered
contract, every dependency with how well it is audited, the open follow-ups,
the dependency results, the run's self-report, and the verify command. Those are six
things a reviewer would otherwise gather from as many commands, and the failure
mode of gathering them by hand is skipping one silently.
{{- if .ReviewRunner }}

**This project splits the two roles.** `drover review <id>` hands the prompt to
`{{ .ReviewRunner }}` rather than printing it, so whoever implemented an item is
not who reviews it. `--runner` overrides that for one pass, and the item's own
frontmatter never selects a reviewer: it names who implements the item, and
reviewing under the same name is the self-certification `audit` refuses to
record as `verified`.
{{- end }}

Then do the part no tool can:

1. **Validate against the code, not the summary.** Confirm each numbered
   requirement against the tree that actually shipped, and against this
   project's principles: {{ .Posture }}
2. **Treat a flagged dependency as unproven.** `review` marks any dependency
   nobody independently confirmed. What it claims to have built is a claim.
3. **Send what you find to its owner; do not absorb it.** Four questions, in this order:
   does a numbered requirement reach it, would it break something in use, can
   you correct it here without a verify command noticing, and does any existing item own
   it. `git diff <trunk>...HEAD` names what the run answers for, and that
   answers who caused a defect rather than whether a pull request should stay
   open. Read every destination before choosing one; the first row a finding
   matches is not always the row that owns it, and picking wrong costs in both
   directions. A backlog fills with rows nothing closes, or a branch collects
   round after round over findings nobody would have scheduled.
{{- range .Destinations }}

   `{{ .Token }}` > {{ .Where }}
{{ .Wrap 6 }}
{{- end }}

   `FOLLOW-UP-TO-OWNER` is the only one that reaches the tracker. `NEW-ITEM`
   writes a body in this project's backlog and reaches no tracker at all, and
   `FIX-IN-REVIEW` reaches neither. `FOLLOW-UP-TO-OWNER` goes there labeled,
   with its owner as a link rather than a label:
   - follow-up carried forward > labels `{{ .FollowUpLabel }}` +
     `{{ .SeverityPrefix }}<critical|high|medium|low>`, filed as a sub-issue of
     the owning item's issue (`gh issue create --parent <n>`) when that item
     names one, otherwise with an `Owner: <owning id>` line in the body. Never an
     owner label: drover reads none.
   - a concrete reproducible defect worth its own unit of work > your tracker's
     bug label, then `drover intake <n>`

   Ownership travels **one hop**: a prompt reads what the item itself owns plus
   what each id on its `deps:` line owns, nothing further. `drover unaudited`
   walks the graph transitively; prompts deliberately do not, because carrying
   every ancestor's open gaps would bury the ones the author can act on. Confirm
   the run you expect to close it lists that owner as a direct dependency:
   `drover run <id> --dry-run | grep -A20 'Open follow-ups'`. A follow-up with no
   owner link reaches nobody.
4. **Discharge what this branch already fixed.** Everything above points
   outward; this is the hop back. A follow-up owned here from an earlier pass is named
   on no `closes:` key, and `closes:` is the only list `finish` reads, so a row
   this branch fixed closes nothing and comments nothing. Whoever filed it gets
   silence. `review` lists the rows and pre-fills the command:

   `drover resolve <id> <n> <fixed|partial|unrelated> --code-by <who> --link "<what shipped>"`

   `--link` widens the key first, which is what a follow-up filed after the item
   was written needs. Record nothing for a row the branch did not touch:
   undisposed keeps it open and owned, and `finish` reports it orphaned.
5. **Write anything going back to the branch into the item body**, with
   `drover request-changes <id> -f <file>`. That body is the whole prompt `run` assembles:
   a report and an audit note both reach the operator and neither reaches the
   next run, so a gap recorded only there is one the next run rediscovers or
   ships again. Give it the file and line, the command that reproduces it and
   its actual output, the shape of the fix, and the test that would fail today.
   `request-changes` replaces the section rather than appending, so say which findings are
   now settled; it refuses to change the numbered requirements, which are the
   contract you are auditing against.

   **A requirement the code cannot meet as written goes to the operator with
   `drover request-changes <id> -f <file> --operator N[,N...]`**, its reason
   under a heading naming the requirement: one that depends on a release
   upstream never made, a decision nobody has made, or evidence no source
   publishes. Sent back as an ordinary finding, the implementer cannot discharge
   it and the loop spends rounds until it parks anyway. drive parks the item
   `needs_decision` and launches no further run for it; the operator amends the
   contract, or tags it `[operator]` and signs it off.

   **A property the code must keep goes in with `drover invariant <id> -f <file>`
   instead**, which appends and is never replaced. A finding names the case that
   broke; an invariant names what may not break again, and every later pass reads
   it. Five rounds of fixes each aimed at the reported case is what happens when
   only the first channel exists.

   **A block this branch closed gets cleared with `drover close-changes <id>`**, which
   replaces the section with the statement that it is closed and writes no
   ledger row. Leave one standing and the next run reads settled gaps as current
   instructions; request changes on "nothing came back" instead and the row that
   writes falls inside this review, where `open-pr` refuses the branch it just cleared
   and `[review] max_review_rounds` counts the round. One disposition per pass: `close-changes`
   refuses while this pass's own changes row stands.
{{- if .MergeGate }}
6. **Leave the full verify command to the merge gate.** The implementing session
   ran it before committing, and every merge waits on `{{ .MergeGate }}`, which
   refuses a head that is not green. Build, and run the tests of only the
   packages this diff touches. This project's verify commands, for reference:
{{- else }}
6. **Re-run the verify command yourself.** `review` prints the exact command. This
   project's verify commands:
{{- end }}

   | When | Command |
   | --- | --- |
{{- range .VerifyCommands }}
   | {{ if eq .Name "default" }}no flag set{{ else }}`{{ .Name }}: yes`{{ end }} | `{{ .Command }}` |
{{- end }}

   A verify command named for a frontmatter key wins when that key is `yes`. Names are
   tried in sorted order and the first match wins, so a flagged verify command must be a
   superset of the default: drover selects one verify command, it never composes several,
   and a check living in only one of them stops running the moment two flags are
   set together.
7. **Record what you checked.** `review` prints this pre-filled:

   `drover audit <id> <claimed|reviewed|verified> --code-by <who> [--reviewer <name>] "<note>"`

   `--reviewer` is optional and nothing defaults it, so omitting it records the
   same absence as nobody having looked. Name yourself there when a person read the
   diff; a session drover launched is already on the row `review` wrote.

   `verified` means every numbered requirement was traced to shipped code,
   verify commands included. Finding one unmet does **not** lower the level: the row
   records how hard you looked, not how the item scored. What happens to the gap
   depends on whether the work can still be changed. Before the merge the three
   `BRANCH-` cases above go back through a request for changes and `FIX-IN-REVIEW` is
   corrected in this pass; either way nothing is filed. A defect found after
   the merge is a bug: the branch is gone, so it is filed through intake (your
   tracker's bug label, then `drover intake <n>`) or with
   `drover new <kind> "<title>" -f <file>` without `--found-in`, and the row
   still records that someone traced them all.
   Drop to `reviewed` only when you could not trace one.

   **`--code-by` is not a formality.** It names who wrote the code under
   review: `agent` for a session drover launched, a person's name, or a comma
   list (`agent,sam`) for mixed authorship. `agent` resolves per session
   against the item's stage rows, so a review session drover launched is
   independent of the implement session it follows. drover **refuses**
   `verified` and exits 3 when you match an entry, because whoever wrote the
   code cannot certify every requirement against it. A `FIX-IN-REVIEW`
   correction no numbered requirement traces to is not code the level
   certifies, so it does not make this session an author of it; anything else
   does. If you ran the item and are now reviewing it, the honest level is
   `claimed` or `reviewed`, and someone else records `verified` later.

   The **most recent** row wins, not the strongest, so a level can be corrected
   in either direction and `drover audit` prints `(DOWNGRADED from …)` when it
   lowers one. The note is the value: name what you re-checked and what you
   found, not that you looked. If you could not fully check the item, leave the
   level where it is and say why — an over-claimed `verified` is worse than
   `UNAUDITED`, which is at least honest about what nobody knows.
{{- if .MergeDrivePR }}
8. **Merge only what this project lets a supervisor merge.** A session drover
   launched as an agent never merges and never marks an item done, and never
   opens a pull request: `open-pr` is the operator's, and an open one is the
   claim that this branch may merge. This project sets `[supervise]
   merge_drive_pr = "supervisor"`, so a supervising session
   (`DROVER_ACTOR=supervisor`) may: {{ .MergeDo }}
{{- if .MergeDenyPaths }}
   A pull request that changes a `[drive] protected_paths` file is one of these too,
   once the supervisor has read the diff of each denied file and named each in
   its report before merging.
{{- else }}
   A pull request that changes a `[drive] protected_paths` file stays the operator's.
{{- end }}
   Everything else stops at the merge point: code the session wrote,
   a pull request drive did not open, a red or pending check. Report the item
   ready and let the operator make it.
{{- else }}
8. **Stop at the merge point.** Do not merge and do not mark it done. Do not open
   a pull request either: `open-pr` is the operator's, and an open one is the
   claim that this branch may merge. Report the item ready and let them make it.
{{- end }}

## After the merge
{{ if .Git }}
{{ if .MergeDrivePR }}The operator, or a supervising session under `merge_drive_pr`, merges, then
runs `drover finish [id]`, which appends the completion{{ else }}The operator merges, then runs `drover finish [id]`, which appends the completion{{ end }}
row to `{{ .Ledger }}`, saves the result under `{{ .Reports }}/`, and returns
the tree to the trunk. With no id it resolves the item from the checked-out
branch.

`mark-merged` is the subset of it: the ledger row alone, for a merge recorded by hand.
Running that first does not block `finish`, which skips the row it finds and does
the rest.

`finish` refuses on a branch it cannot see merged, and exits 3. Work that merged
under a branch name the template does not render (a hand-named branch, or an
item filed after the fact) needs `--force`, or `mark-merged` plus `audit` if you are
not on the tree anyway.

That name comes from `[run] branch`, which this project renders as:

```
{{ .Branch }}
```

Nothing parses it back into an id. Every command that needs an item's branch
renders this template again, so a branch created under an older one is a branch
drover no longer looks for. `drover show <id>` prints the name an item resolves
to today.

`finish` deliberately does not audit. Finishing is mechanical; auditing is judgement,
and a command that did both would let the thing that merged the work also
certify it.
{{- else }}
This project sets `[git] enabled = false`. There are no branches and no merge,
so `finish` refuses and exits 3. The operator records completion with `drover mark-merged
[id]`, which appends the row to `{{ .Ledger }}`, and records its level with
`drover audit [id] <level> --code-by <who> [--reviewer <name>]`.

Work in place. Nothing switches branches before a run, and nothing verifies a
merge after one.
{{- end }}

## Writing an item

- **A mechanical change is one item, and its requirement is a script.** A
  rename, a reformat, a file move or a dependency bump is a term map applied by
  a script in one branch, plus the build and test fixes it causes, under one
  review. Splitting it into parts multiplies the implementing and reviewing
  sessions and makes no part easier to check: drover's own six-item rename took
  a day that one scripted pass would have done in hours.
- **Keep new behavior out of a mechanical item.** A flag that changes what a
  command does, a new field, a new rule: each is its own item with its own
  requirements. Mixed into a sweep, the review argues design while it is meant
  to be checking that nothing was missed.
- **A change to a stored format carries its migration.** An item that renames
  a config key, a ledger field or a stored value ships the script that rewrites
  existing data, with a test that runs it on a sample, in the same branch.
  "The operator migrates it later" leaves the migration for whoever installs
  the release, written under deadline.
- **State the cost before queuing several items.** Say how many items, how many
  sessions each, and the time you expect, so the operator can choose a cheaper
  route before the queue spends the day.
- **The numbered list is the contract.** `report --save` turns every top-level
  `N. ` line into an audit checkbox, so a requirement written as prose vanishes
  from the checklist and nobody will ever confirm it.
- **Name files and lines.** An item that says "improve the handler" produces a
  run that improves something; one that cites `path/to/file.go:120-145` produces
  a run that can be checked.
- **Cite files, not directories.** Under `drive --jobs` a directory in
  backticks holds the item apart from every item citing anything below it, and
  the dispatcher cannot tell a directory named for context from one the item
  will edit. Name a directory that is context only in plain prose, without
  backticks and without a file name after it.
{{- if .StopMarker }}
- **Scaffolded items carry the marker `{{ .StopMarker }}`** and drover refuses
  to launch or even preview them until a human writes the requirements.
  Requirements lifted mechanically from a bug report are checkboxes nobody can
  falsify.
{{- if .DraftRunner }}
- **`drover draft <id>` hands the drafting prompt to `{{ .DraftRunner }}`**: the
  issue read fresh from the tracker, the principles above, the numbered-list
  contract, the rest of the backlog for finding owners, and the body file to write
  into. The draft leaves the marker in place. Removing it is the operator
  accepting the requirements, and no flag on `draft` does it for them.
{{- else }}
- **`drover draft <id>` assembles the prompt for writing them** and prints it.
  Set `[run] draft_runner` to hand it to an agent instead. Either way the draft
  leaves the marker in place: removing it is the operator accepting the
  requirements, and no flag on `draft` does it for them.
{{- end }}
- **`drover accept <id>` is that removal, and `drover unaccept <id>` reverses
  it.** Both are the operator's to run. Neither is reachable from a runner's
  argv, which is the whole reason accepting is a command and not a flag.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Specs }}
- **`spec:` tokens resolve against `{{ .Specs }}/`.** A token naming a file that
  is not there is carried into the prompt verbatim, so check what a run will
  actually be told to read.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Skills }}
- **`skills:` tokens must be vendored under `{{ .Skills }}/<name>/SKILL.md`.**
  Run `drover skills` after adding one: a referenced guide that is not vendored
  degrades to a suggestion an unattended run cannot read.
{{- end }}

## Doctrine lives in the templates, not here

{{ if .Implement }}`{{ .Implement }}` holds what a run is told{{ else }}The built-in prompt template holds what a run is told{{ end }}{{ if .Draft }}, `{{ .Draft }}` holds what a draft is told{{ end }}{{ if .Review }}, and `{{ .Review }}` holds what a review is told{{ end }}.
Change what an agent is handed by editing {{ if .Implement }}those files{{ else }}`[prompts] implement`{{ end }}, not this
document: a template reaches the item, its dependencies, the verify command and the open
follow-ups, so it applies per item rather than per reader who remembers to look.
